---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Challenges with WebAssembly — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. It compiled quickly. Getting the colours right, reading files reliably and shipping it without breaks took me much longer."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Challenges with WebAssembly</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The same Rust code runs in a browser tab. It compiled quickly.
          Getting the colours right, reading files reliably and shipping it
          without breaks took me much longer.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          The <a href="/app.html">web version</a> of LightPhotos is the Rust
          code of the desktop app, compiled to WebAssembly. It works in desktop
          Chrome and Edge. The compile was easy. The hard bugs appeared when
          the app ran: in the clock, threads, crashes, colours, file access and
          deploys. A few of the fixes changed the desktop app too.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="wasm-colour">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <span>Colour path:</span>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="bug" aria-pressed="true">First browser build</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="fix" aria-pressed="false">Now: shaders convert</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 204" role="img" aria-label="The colour path from the shader to the monitor, and the values that three gray test bars got on the screen.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="8" width="190" height="46" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="12" y="28">Shader output</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="45">linear light</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="190" y1="31" x2="215" y2="31"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M215 26 L225 31 L215 36 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" data-el="conv-box" x="225" y="8" width="190" height="46" rx="5"></rect>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" data-el="conv-dot" cx="401" cy="22" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" data-el="conv-t" x="237" y="28">No conversion</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-el="conv-s" x="237" y="45">the WebGPU canvas has none</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="415" y1="31" x2="440" y2="31"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M440 26 L450 31 L440 36 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="450" y="8" width="190" height="46" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="462" y="28">Monitor</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="462" y="45">expects sRGB values</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="86">GRAY TEST BARS: VALUE ON SCREEN, 0 TO 255</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="113">target 64</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-line" x="80" y="99" width="118" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad" data-bar="0" x="80" y="99" width="24" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t lp-fig-mono" data-val="0" x="448" y="114">13</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="145">target 128</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-line" x="80" y="131" width="236" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad" data-bar="1" x="80" y="131" width="101" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t lp-fig-mono" data-val="1" x="448" y="146">55</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="177">target 192</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-line" x="80" y="163" width="354" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad" data-bar="2" x="80" y="163" width="247" height="20" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t lp-fig-mono" data-val="2" x="448" y="178">134</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="80" y="200">outline: the correct value</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">A 50% gray (128) reached the screen as 55, approximately 21%. Every midtone was too dark.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Switch between the first browser build and the current one. The
          compiler caught none of this.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>How the browser version is laid out</h2>
      <p>
        The browser version is two programs. The main one runs the
        interface and the graphics on the main thread. A smaller one runs in
        Web Workers, background threads with no window, and decodes RAW files
        and renders exports.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="The browser build. The main thread of the page runs the interface and draws with WebGPU. It reads and writes your folder through the File System Access API. It sends decode and export jobs to a maximum of four Web Workers. Each worker is a separate copy of a smaller WebAssembly program with its own memory. Downloads, compressed: approximately 3.2 MB for the main program and 1.1 MB for the worker.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE BROWSER TAB</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="28" width="250" height="72" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="50">Main thread</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="68">interface, catalog, WebGPU drawing</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="86">12 MB wasm, 3.2 MB compressed</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="250" y1="64" x2="300" y2="64"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M300 59 L310 64 L300 69 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="256" y="56">jobs</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="310" y="28" width="330" height="116" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="326" y="50">Up to 4 Web Workers</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="326" y="62" width="70" height="30" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="361" y="81" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="404" y="62" width="70" height="30" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="439" y="81" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="482" y="62" width="70" height="30" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="517" y="81" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="560" y="62" width="70" height="30" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="595" y="81" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="326" y="112">each its own copy, its own memory</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="326" y="130">RAW decode, export rendering</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="125" y1="100" x2="125" y2="150"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M120 150 L130 150 L125 160 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="160" width="250" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="180">Your folder</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="198">File System Access API, no paths</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="180">No shared memory, no special server headers.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="198">Workers get bytes in and send pixels back.</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A tool called trunk builds it. One setting for the graphics code
        never reaches the compiler from the trunk config file, so every build
        script sets it as an environment variable instead.
      </p>

      <h2>It compiled, but it did not run</h2>
      <p>
        The compile showed the expected gaps: no system trash, no clipboard,
        no random numbers until a library uses the browser source, and no
        threads. These were easy. Then I opened the app in a browser, and it
        crashed. The compiler could not catch any of these:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Asking for the time crashed the app.</strong>
          <code>Instant::now()</code> has no browser implementation and
          crashes instead of returning an error. Now every timer, and a
          patched copy of the RAW decoder, uses a small library that asks the
          clock of the browser.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>I could not wait on the main thread.</strong> The desktop
          app waits for the graphics card before it shows a window. A browser
          page must give control back, or nothing happens. Now the app checks
          on every frame whether graphics setup is done.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The window started at 0&times;0 pixels</strong> until the
          browser reported its size, and WebGPU rejects every drawing command
          outside the canvas. On a high-resolution screen, my clip rectangles
          were also twice too big.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebGPU is stricter than Metal.</strong> One shader stopped
          early for some pixels and then read from a texture. Metal and Vulkan
          allowed that. WebGPU refused to compile it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The colours were wrong</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, the app gives the graphics card linear light, and the
        system converts it to the sRGB values that a monitor expects. A WebGPU
        canvas never converts, so every midtone was far too dark, as the demo
        at the top shows. Now the shaders convert on every platform, including
        the desktop, so the two builds cannot drift apart. Decoding still
        differs: Apple ImageIO on macOS, the app's own decoders everywhere
        else.
      </p>

      <h2>Threads without shared memory</h2>
      <p>
        Before the port, I wrote down a plan. It got memory right and
        threading completely wrong, and I know that only because I wrote it
        down. It predicted shared memory, two special security headers and a
        library that runs the Rust thread pool in the browser. That library
        expects JavaScript to start first. LightPhotos is a normal Rust
        program with its own <code>main()</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        So each worker runs its own copy of the worker program. The main
        thread sends it file bytes and gets pixels back. No special headers,
        no experimental Rust. The cost is memory, so the pool stops at four
        workers. A tab is 32-bit, and its memory never shrinks after it grows.
        See <a href="/blogs/measuring-a-gui-app.html">measuring a GUI app</a>
        and <a href="/blogs/never-load-the-whole-folder.html">never load the
        whole folder</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>No recovery after a crash</h2>
      <p>
        In the browser, a crash in any library is my problem. On the desktop,
        if the RAW decoder panics on an unusual file, the app catches the
        panic and shows a placeholder. In the browser, a panic kills the
        worker. So the app checks each file before the decoder gets it:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>It rejects file layouts that hit a <code>todo!()</code> in the decoder.</li>
        <li>It rejects Fujifilm X-Trans files, which hit an <code>unreachable!()</code>.</li>
        <li>It checks width times height for overflow, which a big RAW file can cause in the 32-bit build but never on a 64-bit Mac.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When it can, the worker uses the JPEG preview inside the RAW file
        instead. That is faster, and it cannot crash.
      </p>

      <h2>Files, one handle at a time</h2>
      <p>
        The File System Access API lets the page read and write inside a
        folder that you pick. It gives only handles, never paths, and every
        operation is asynchronous. Two errors took me the longest:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong><code>NotReadableError</code>.</strong> Chrome throws it
          when too many reads run at once. Two never caused it. The app allows
          four, and retries after a random delay that grows each time. Fixed
          retries failed again, because all the reads retried at the same
          moment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>RangeError: Array buffer allocation failed</code>.</strong>
          The app copied each RAW file into JavaScript and again into
          WebAssembly memory, and the tab ran out. Now there is no extra copy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You must still pick the folder again each time you open the page.
        Deletes are permanent, because the API has no trash. The app warns
        you first.
      </p>

      <h2>Deploying broke it</h2>
      <p>
        The worst bug was not in the Rust code. How I deploy matters as much
        as the code. Cloudflare cached the worker JavaScript for four hours
        and its WebAssembly for zero. Recent visitors got old JavaScript with
        new WebAssembly. Every decode failed because a function did not exist.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="wasm-cache">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <label>Hours after a release <input type="range" min="0" max="6" step="0.5" value="1"></label>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-hash="off" aria-pressed="true">Same file addresses</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-hash="on" aria-pressed="false">Build hash in addresses</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 150" role="img" aria-label="Which build of each worker file a visitor gets from the Cloudflare cache, by hours after a release.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHAT A VISITOR GETS FROM THE CACHE</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="44">Worker JS</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="60">cached 4 hours</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" data-el="js-old" x="150" y="28" width="320" height="36" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-el="js-old-t" x="162" y="50">old build</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" data-el="js-new" x="470" y="28" width="160" height="36" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" data-el="js-new-t" x="482" y="50">new build</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="90">Worker wasm</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="106">cached 0 hours</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="150" y="74" width="480" height="36" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="162" y="96">new build</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" data-el="marker" x1="230" y1="20" x2="230" y2="118" stroke-width="2"></line>
          <g class="lp-fig-s">
            <text x="150" y="138" text-anchor="middle">0 h</text>
            <text x="230" y="138" text-anchor="middle">1 h</text>
            <text x="310" y="138" text-anchor="middle">2 h</text>
            <text x="390" y="138" text-anchor="middle">3 h</text>
            <text x="470" y="138" text-anchor="middle">4 h</text>
            <text x="550" y="138" text-anchor="middle">5 h</text>
            <text x="630" y="138" text-anchor="end">6 h</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">1 h: old worker JS with new wasm. Every decode fails.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Move the slider, then add the build hash. With new addresses, the
          cache has no old copy to give.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The fix adds the build hash of the main bundle, a fingerprint of the
        build, to the worker file
        addresses, so every release gets new addresses. When I moved the app
        under <code>/app/</code>, the worker address broke too. Now the worker
        finds its own location from the page.
      </p>

      <h2>What the browser version gives up</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>It works only in desktop Chrome and Edge, which have WebGPU and folder access.</li>
        <li>No HEIC, no face detection and no subject selection.</li>
        <li>No Immich upload, because Immich servers do not accept requests from web pages.</li>
        <li>No clipboard. Exports always go to an <code>Exports</code> folder inside the folder that you picked.</li>
        <li>You download approximately 4.3&nbsp;MB, compressed, before the app starts.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Was it worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. A link is easier to send than an installer. The port also made
        the desktop app better: shared shader colour conversion, thread pools
        that do not panic when they cannot start, and a thumbnail cache next
        to your photos. If the <a href="/app.html">web app</a> breaks for
        you, tell me your browser and what you did on the <a
        href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/my-plan.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">My plan, and what is not in it</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/exporting-to-your-own-immich-server.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Exporting to your own Immich server</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    // Colour demo: values are the gray test bars from the post (64, 128, 192 read 13, 55, 134).
    const colour = document.getElementById("wasm-colour");
    if (colour) {
      const targets = [64, 128, 192];
      const onScreen = { bug: [13, 55, 134], fix: targets };
      const scale = 118 / 64;
      const el = (name) => colour.querySelector(`[data-el="${name}"]`);
      const readout = colour.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const show = (mode) => {
        const bug = mode === "bug";
        colour.querySelectorAll("[data-mode]").forEach((b) =>
          b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b.dataset.mode === mode)));
        el("conv-box").setAttribute("class", bug ? "lp-fig-box" : "lp-fig-box-accent");
        el("conv-dot").style.opacity = bug ? "1" : "0";
        el("conv-t").textContent = bug ? "No conversion" : "Shader converts";
        el("conv-s").textContent = bug ? "the WebGPU canvas has none" : "same code on every platform";
        onScreen[mode].forEach((v, i) => {
          const bar = colour.querySelector(`[data-bar="${i}"]`);
          bar.setAttribute("width", String(Math.round(v * scale)));
          bar.setAttribute("class", bug ? "lp-fig-bad" : "lp-fig-bar");
          colour.querySelector(`[data-val="${i}"]`).textContent = String(v);
        });
        readout.textContent = bug
          ? "A 50% gray (128) reached the screen as 55, approximately 21%. Every midtone was too dark."
          : "A 50% gray (128) reaches the screen as 128. The shader does the sRGB conversion.";
      };
      colour.querySelectorAll("[data-mode]").forEach((b) =>
        b.addEventListener("click", () => show(b.dataset.mode)));
    }

    // Cache demo: Cloudflare kept the worker JS for 4 hours and the wasm for 0.
    const cache = document.getElementById("wasm-cache");
    if (cache) {
      const range = cache.querySelector("input[type=range]");
      const readout = cache.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const el = (name) => cache.querySelector(`[data-el="${name}"]`);
      const x0 = 150, perHour = 80, jsHours = 4;
      let hashed = false;
      const update = () => {
        const h = Number(range.value);
        const stale = !hashed && h < jsHours;
        cache.querySelectorAll("[data-hash]").forEach((b) =>
          b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String((b.dataset.hash === "on") === hashed)));
        el("js-old").style.opacity = hashed ? "0" : "1";
        el("js-old-t").style.opacity = hashed ? "0" : "1";
        el("js-new").setAttribute("x", String(hashed ? x0 : x0 + jsHours * perHour));
        el("js-new").setAttribute("width", String(hashed ? 480 : 160));
        el("js-new-t").setAttribute("x", String((hashed ? x0 : x0 + jsHours * perHour) + 12));
        el("js-new-t").textContent = hashed ? "new build, new address" : "new build";
        const x = x0 + h * perHour;
        el("marker").setAttribute("x1", String(x));
        el("marker").setAttribute("x2", String(x));
        el("marker").setAttribute("class", stale ? "lp-fig-bad-stroke" : "lp-fig-line");
        readout.textContent = stale
          ? `${h} h: old worker JS with new wasm. Every decode fails.`
          : `${h} h: worker JS and wasm come from the same build. Decodes work.`;
      };
      range.addEventListener("input", update);
      cache.querySelectorAll("[data-hash]").forEach((b) =>
        b.addEventListener("click", () => { hashed = b.dataset.hash === "on"; update(); }));
    }
  </script>
</Base>
